This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation.

We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min\left\{\mathcal{L}u,\mathcal{H}u,\psi\right\}=0\mbox{ in }B_1\subset\mathbb{R}^n,
  \end{equation*}
  where the operator $\mathcal{L}$ takes the form
  \begin{equation*}
  \mathcal{D}_t p D_xp+\mathcal{H}(x,D_x,D_tp,u,D_xuD_xu),
  \end{equation*}
  with the function $f$ exhibiting 2-sobolev growth relative to the gradient, assuming minimal growth conditions.
